What the element wildcard has to get right on the dynamic path.

A wildcard prop is a map of the names it collected, not a name of its own.
Three sites turn props into attributes -- static markup, the route-mode
skeleton, and the DOM writes a node inside a `for` or a reactive slot takes.
The third wrote the map itself, so a looped checkbox got an attribute called
"attrs" holding "[object Map]" and no type at all, and rendered as a text box.

Nothing else in testdata reaches this path, so the html corpus generates
identically with the fix in or out; this loop is the only thing holding it.

deny out/none/html/index.html `setAttribute\("attrs"` -- "attrs" is the collection, not an attribute a browser knows: the map is unpacked into one setAttribute per name it collected
deny out/none/html/index.html `object Map` -- nothing stringifies the map, which is what writing it straight to an attribute produces

-- static markup, the route-mode
skeleton, and the DOM writes a node inside a `for` or a reactive slot
takes. The third wrote the map itself, so a looped checkbox got an
attribute called "attrs" holding "[object Map]" and no type at all, and
rendered as a text box.

Nothing in testdata reaches this path, so the html corpus generates
identically with the fix in or out.

The map is unpacked into one attribute per name it collected.
-- loop.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var items = ["a", "b"]
    var on = false
    vbox {
        for var it = items {
            checkbox(label=it, checked=on)
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
